{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789391921,"nonce":"v/FmRxpdDRYTjQB1JOvotXa0CVQ=","title":"Pool allocator: a thread-local lookup per alloc and per free is a third of binary_trees","message":"Sampling bench_binary_trees.py at depth 19: of the main thread's\nsamples, 33% are in `_tlv_get_addr` and 20% in `zyntax_alloc` itself;\nthe compiled code is the other half. Every `zyntax_alloc` and\n`zyntax_free` reaches its free list through `thread_local!`, which on\nmacOS is a call into dyld per access. The pool is otherwise a push and\na pop, so the lookup is most of what an allocation costs, and it costs\nZynML and ZyPy alike (212 and 263 ms at depth 16).\n\nOptions, none free: a process-wide free list is only correct while one\nthread allocates (parallel bands and M:N fibers allocate from\nworkers); a pool pointer cached per compiled function and passed to\nthe intrinsics would amortise one lookup over a body's allocations but\nis wrong across a fiber migration; a per-thread arena the compiled\ncode indexes by a cheap thread id needs a thread id cheaper than the\nTLV call, which on macOS means reading TPIDRRO_EL0 directly.","files":null}]}